# Most commonly used requirements-elicitation technique (Q9_ML_Elicitation_*)

## Answer / hypothesis

Interviews are the most commonly used technique for eliciting requirements in ML-enabled system projects: 97 of the 194 respondents who answered the Q9 elicitation block (50.0%, 95% bootstrap CI 42.8-57.2%) reported using interviews, more than scenarios or prototyping (75 each, 38.7%), workshops/meetings (74, 38.1%), observation (64, 33.0%) or any other technique (11, 5.7%). Interviews rank first on every defensible denominator (188, 194, 276, 174, 170 rows) and in 2000/2000 random subsamples, and they significantly outwork workshops/meetings, observation and 'others' in paired McNemar tests (Holm-adjusted p<0.05); the lead over scenarios/prototyping is directionally consistent but only marginal after multiplicity correction (Holm p~0.056). The 69.399% (95% CI [69.062, 69.735]) figure quoted in the question is NOT reproducible from this export and is reported only as a labelled discrepancy reference.

## Coding decisions (fixed before the run; no filter tuned to any target figure)

* Export is pre-coded: each Q9 item holds `quoted` / `not quoted` / `-77`. **No Likert or intensity scale exists in this file**, so no top-2-box or mean-intensity ranking is possible.
* indicator `uses = (value=='quoted')`; `-77` (item block never reached, 82/276 rows, all `Status = Suspended`) is missing and excluded listwise at respondent level.
* primary denominator = 194 respondents exposed to the Q9 block (incl. 6 Suspended rows that contain a complete block); sensitivity frames 188 / 276 / 174 / 170.
* bootstrap: B=10000 respondent-row resamples (whole rows, preserving the multi-select correlation), seed=12345, percentile 2.5/97.5 primary, BCa secondary.
* `rank` breaks ties by count then label; `rank_named5` uses min-rank so the Scenarios=Prototyping tie shares rank 2 there.

