## Discrepancy note on the benchmark figure 69.399% (95% CI [69.062, 69.735])

1. **Counts required vs observed.** At n=194 the share is quantised in steps of 0.5155 pp (1/194); 69.399% would need k=135 endorsements (135/194=69.588%) or k=134 (69.072%) - observed interviews count is 97. No frame in this file (276, 194, 190, 188, 174, 164) admits a count giving 69.399%; the only exact match, 127/183, has no counterpart among the Q9 counts.

| denominator_N | k_needed_for_69_399pct | realised_pct_of_that_k | exact_match | observed_interviews_quoted | observed_pct |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 276 | 192 | 69.5652 | False | 97.0 | 35.145 |
| 194 | 135 | 69.5876 | False | 97.0 | 50.0 |
| 190 | 132 | 69.4737 | False | nan | nan |
| 188 | 130 | 69.1489 | False | 95.0 | 50.532 |
| 183 | 127 | 69.3989 | True | nan | nan |
| 174 | 121 | 69.5402 | False | 97.0 | 55.747 |
| 164 | 114 | 69.5122 | False | nan | nan |
| 140 | 97 | 69.2857 | False | nan | nan |

2. **The quoted CI width cannot come from a respondent-level proportion here.** Half-width 0.3365 pp implies an effective Bernoulli N ~ 72,047 (Wald inversion), 371x the primary base; our bootstrap CI width is 14.43 pp and the smallest theoretically possible Wald width at n=276 is 5.44 pp. Also, a proportion on n=194 can only take values on a 0.5155 pp grid, so bounds quoted to 0.001 pp are impossible.

| diagnostic | value |
|---|---|
| benchmark point estimate (%) | 69.399 |
| benchmark CI half-width (pp) | 0.3365 |
| implied effective N for Bernoulli proportion (Wald inversion) | 72046.8 |
| largest possible n in this file | 276.0 |
| implied_N / 194 (primary base) | 371.4 |
| bootstrap CI width (pp) we actually obtain, Interviews n=194 | 14.433 |
| Wald half-width (pp) at n=194 for p=0.69399 | 6.485 |
| nearest interpretable coincidence: 97/140 (%) | 69.2857 |
| MC SE (pp) of a B=1000 bootstrap mean at n=140, p=97/140 | 0.1233 |

3. **Nearest interpretable reconstruction (hypothesis, not a result):** 97/140 = 69.2857%, i.e. the same 97 interview endorsements on a ~140-respondent valid base; a B=1000 bootstrap mean carries MC SE 0.123 pp, enough to display 69.399%. This suggests the benchmark figure was computed on a different (smaller/earlier) export. It is not used as our estimate.

4. **Post-hoc subsets rejected:** every single-column filter with n>=50 was scanned; only n=59 (69.492%) and n=55 (69.091%) come within 0.5 pp and were rejected as data-driven subgroups.

| filter | n | interviews_pct | abs_gap_pp | judgement |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Q2_ML_Life_Cycle_Difficulty_Model_Deployment==Complex | 59 | 69.492 | 0.0925 | post-hoc, n too small, excluded |
| Q2_ML_Life_Cycle_Difficulty_Model_Monitoring==Complex | 55 | 69.091 | 0.3081 | post-hoc, n too small, excluded |

## Robustness / cross-checks

* All 13 counts and frame sizes reproduce the read-only inspection stage exactly (`q9_verification_vs_inspection.csv`).
* Bootstrap percentile CIs agree with analytic Wilson intervals to <1 pp (cross_checks.py).
* Percentile bounds are identical across 8 alternative seeds (discrete n=194 grid).
* argmax = Interviews in 2000/2000 random 85% row subsamples.
* McNemar p-values reproduced by hand-computed binomial sums (independent of scipy).

## Limitations

* 'Others' endorsements include junk free-text (blank, "I don't know", "Beer please"); it is the last-ranked item either way and the 5-named-technique ranking is also reported.
* `quoted`/`not quoted` cannot distinguish frequency or intensity of use; 'most commonly used' here means 'most often endorsed'.
* 82 of 276 rows never saw the block; they are excluded rather than counted as non-users.
